{"description":"The 2026 Oscars announced a rare tie in the best live‑action short film category, awarding both "The Singers" and "Two People Exchanging Saliva".","articleSummary":"The 2026 Academy Awards stunned viewers by declaring a tie in the best live‑action short film category, making it the seventh tie in Oscar history and the first in that category since 1995.  The announcement was delivered by comedian Kumail Nanjiani, who noted the unusual outcome and then handed the trophies to the two winners, each of whom gave a separate acceptance speech.  The event was highlighted by comedic remarks from hosts such as Conan O’Brien, who quipped about the tie affecting Oscar pools, and other industry personalities, while the winners themselves expressed surprise and admiration for each other.

The article provides context by listing all prior Oscar ties, including the 1932 best‑actor tie between Fredric March and Wallace Beery, the 1950 documentary short tie, the 1969 best‑actress tie between Katharine Hepburn and Barbra Streisand, the 1987 best‑documentary tie, the 1995 live‑action short tie, and the 2013 sound‑editing tie between "Skyfall" and "Zero Dark Thirty."  It also mentions Mark Wahlberg’s role in announcing the 2013 tie and the Academy’s changed rules that previously allowed a nominee to win if within three votes of the leader.

Throughout, the piece focuses on the novelty of the tie, the reactions of the winners and hosts, and the historical record, presenting the event as a lighthearted moment in a long tradition of award ceremonies.","points":["The 2026 Oscars awarded a tie in the best live‑action short film category to "The Singers" and "Two People Exchanging Saliva".","Kumail Nanjiani presented the award and announced the tie during the ceremony.","The tie is the seventh overall in Oscar history and the first live‑action short tie since 1995.","Previous Oscar ties include 1932 best‑actor (Fredric March vs. Wallace Beery), 1950 best documentary short, 1969 best‑actress (Katharine Hepburn vs. Barbra Streisand), 1987 best documentary, 1995 live‑action short, and 2013 best sound editing ("Skyfall" vs. "Zero Dark Thirty").","Conan O’Brien made jokes about the tie affecting Oscar pools, and the winners delivered separate acceptance speeches.","Mark Wahlberg announced the 2013 tie alongside animated character Ted voiced by Seth MacFarlane."]}
